The '''Compañía Real de Minaria de Sarta''' was the crown-owned company created in 1847 to manage Sarta's coal mining operations. It held the main mining monopoly in the kingdom and became the central institution behind the post-1846 industrial expansion.

The company was one of a wider network of royal companies established by the Sartan state between 1846 and 1880 to manage public works and strategic sectors such as road construction, railways, and mining. These companies were semi-autonomous in daily operation, but they remained answerable to parliament and were directly controlled by lords appointed through the upper house.

== Formation ==

The company was formed after the return of the coastal territories in the [[Occidental Agreement of 1846]]. The crown wanted a single institution to organise the expanding coal economy, and the mining company provided a way to coordinate extraction, transport, and export without ceding control to private owners.

Coal production was concentrated in the [[Sierra Madre]], where the new mines served both Sartan industry and Volisanian demand. That made the company highly profitable, but it also placed it at the centre of labour disputes and political debate.

== Operations ==

The company supervised the largest mining sites, set production targets, and coordinated shipments toward the coast. Because it was crown-owned, its directors were chosen through the royal political system rather than by private shareholders. Its semi-autonomous structure allowed it to move quickly, but its accountability to the lordly legislature kept it tied to state policy.

The company became closely associated with the toxic conditions that triggered the mining stoppages of 1854. Workers' protests against unsafe pits, dust, and poor ventilation exposed the limits of the company's management and contributed to the later constitutional reforms of 1855.

== Significance ==

The Compañía Real de Minaria de Sarta was one of the most important institutions of mid-nineteenth-century Sarta. It linked state power, mining wealth, and coastal industrial growth, and it helped shift the kingdom toward a more heavily managed economy. At the same time, it symbolised the way the lords' parliament could control modern industry while still leaving workers with limited influence.

